    "q-130102": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130102",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y)$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,\\pi ).$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,\\pi ) & =2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{2}-\\pi )\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{2}-\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(0,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x-0,\\,y-\\pi ⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
