    "q-36397": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36397",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given the function $f(x,y)=3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(3y+4)\\,i+(3x+6-10y)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(3(2)+4)\\,i+(3(1)+6-10(2))\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i-11\\,j \\\\  & =⟨10,-11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨10,-11⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
