    "q-72403": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72403",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the plane curve $(x-y)^{2}=2x-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2} & =2x-y \\\\ (x-y)^{2}-2x+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}-2x+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,0)={f}_{y}(2,0)i-{f}_{x}(2,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)j \\\\  & =(-2(x-y)+1)i-(2(x-y)-2)j \\\\ t(2,0) & =(-2((2)-(0))+1)\\,i-((2((2)-(0))-2)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-3\\,i-2\\,j.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
